{"text": "So what does the sensor actually hand over? Not a photo. Not even close. This is the raw frame. It's dark. It's grainy. And it has no colour at all, just a brightness reading for every pixel, laid out in a checkerboard, where each square only knows about one of red, green, or blue. Two thirds of the colour information in the final picture was never recorded. It has to be worked out. That job belongs to the image signal processor, a block of the phone's main chip that does nothing else. First, it pulls the mosaic apart into three sheets, red, green, and blue, each one full of holes. Then it fills the holes. For a pixel that only measured green, it looks at the red neighbours on either side, and estimates the red it would have seen. It's an educated guess, but a careful one: the processor looks for edges, and avoids blending across them, so that a sharp line stays sharp, instead of growing coloured fringes. This step is called demosaicing, and it happens for every one of those millions of pixels. Next, colour. The sensor has no idea that the streetlamp is orange and the shade is blue. Your brain corrects for that automatically. A white shirt looks white to you almost anywhere. So the processor has to do the same, estimating the colour of the light, and scaling the three channels until neutral things look neutral. That's white balance. Then, the grain. The processor compares each pixel with its surroundings, works out what's real detail and what's random speckle, and smooths away the speckle, while trying to leave the texture alone. Then tone, contrast, and sharpening. And only now does it look like a photograph. Every one of these steps runs on every frame, thirty or sixty times a second, just to draw the viewfinder. But at night, even this isn't enough.", "alignment": {"characters": ["S", "o", " ", "w", "h", "a", "t", " ", "d", "o", "e", "s", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "s", "e", "n", "s", "o", "r", " ", "a", "c", "t", "u", "a", "l", "l", "y", " ", "h", "a", "